      "Abstract": {
        "CopyrightInformation": "© 2011 Blackwell Publishing Ltd.",
        "AbstractText": [
          {
            "NlmCategory": "BACKGROUND",
            "Label": "BACKGROUND",
            "content": "An association between coeliac disease (CD) and functional gastrointestinal disorders (FGIDs) has at present only been demonstrated in adults."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
            "Label": "AIMS",
            "content": "To assess the prevalence of FGIDs at 1 year and the role of psychological aspects on the development of FGIDs in CD children."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
            "Label": "METHODS",
            "content": "One-hundred consecutive CD children (36M and 64F) were followed up for 1 year. Fifty-six children (25M and 31F) represented the control group. All children and/or their parents completed validated questionnaires for GI symptoms, depression, and anxiety. GI symptoms at diagnosis and after 1 year of gluten-free diet (GFD) were compared."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
            "Label": "RESULTS",
            "content": "Twenty-three/82 (28%) CD patients followed up prospectively, on GFD from at least 1 year, fulfilled the Rome III criteria for FGIDs compared with 5/56 (8.9%) controls (P = 0.008; χ² = 6.8; OR: 3.97; 95% CI: 1.40-11.21). Children complaining with GI symptoms alone [21/52 (40.3%)] more likely fulfilled Rome III criteria for FGIDs after 1 year of GFD than children with extra-intestinal symptoms (P = 0.045). CD children with FGDIs presented significantly higher anxiety and depression compared to CD children without FGIDs and controls (P = 0.02)."
          },
          {
            "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "Label": "CONCLUSIONS",
            "content": "This study demonstrates that children with CD on a GFD for a year have a much higher prevalence of functional GI symptoms than do controls. Whether the risk is due to the residua of a chronic inflammatory process, and/or due to psychological factors remains to be further tested."
          }
        ]
      },
